<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-worked-examples">
  <title>Worked Examples</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Apply the same analysis sequence to clear and difficult responses.</li>
      <li>Separate extraction, matching, correctness, and goal coverage.</li>
      <li>Recognize when a response should remain incomplete, OOV, no-match, or unresolved.</li>
      <li>Write brief decisions that are tied to visible evidence.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Worked examples expose the decisions a coder must make. Each example follows
      the sequence in <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>: screening,
      extraction, matching, correctness, goal coverage, and the remaining human
      decision. The analyses describe only the justification visible in the
      response.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-examples-access-status">
      <title>Access During Training</title>
      <p>
        This chapter may be used during guided onboarding and remediation. Do not
        consult it during a blind or independent calibration pass unless the
        assigned worksheet explicitly permits access.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      Candidate matches are described by control-structure type rather than by a
      fixed code ID. A final code must come from the current question-specific
      working codebook described in
      <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-example-common-context">
    <title>Common Question Context</title>
    <p>
      Examples 1, 2, 3, 7, 8, and 9 use the following constructed version of the
      source journal's recurring linear-system question. A student is shown the reduced
      row echelon form
      <md>
        \left[
        \begin{array}{ccc|c}
          1 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 3\\
          0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; -1
        \end{array}
        \right]
      </md>
      for a consistent system with variables <m>x_1,x_2,x_3</m>. The prompt asks the
      student to:
      <ol>
        <li>determine whether the solution is unique; and</li>
        <li>identify the basic and free variables.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      The normative mathematical reference is:
      <ul>
        <li><m>x_1</m> and <m>x_3</m> are basic variables;</li>
        <li><m>x_2</m> is a free variable;</li>
        <li>the solution is not unique; and</li>
        <li>because the system is consistent, it has infinitely many solutions.</li>
      </ul>
      The required question goals are the uniqueness decision and the identification
      of the basic and free variables. Infinitely many solutions is a valid consequence,
      but the constructed prompt does not require that phrase explicitly.
    </p>

    <p>
      Examples 4, 5, and 6 use different or incompletely preserved question contexts.
      That limitation is stated when it affects matching or correctness. A response
      cannot be scored responsibly by importing the common context into an unrelated
      example.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="sec-example-template">
      <title>Analysis Format</title>

      <p>
        Every example uses the following fields. The fields correspond to distinct
        decisions and should not be collapsed into a single judgment that the response
        is simply <q>right</q> or <q>wrong.</q>
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-example-analysis-format">
        <title>Fields used in each worked example.</title>
        <tabular top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="28%" halign="center" />
          <col width="72%" halign="left" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Question answered</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Screening</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Is there enough here for episode analysis?</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Extraction</p></cell>
            <cell><p>What episode components are identifiable?</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Matching</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Which codebook codes apply to this response?</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Correctness</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Is the criterion true and the link valid?</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>Goal coverage</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Are all parts of the full question addressed?</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><m>\text{Human decision}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p>What must be confirmed/flagged/escalated?</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-examples-progression">
    <title>Examples</title>

    <example xml:id="example-one-complete">
      <title>One Complete Episode</title>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              There is a free variable, so the solution is not unique.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>

      </statement>
      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>
        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                <c>include</c>. A mathematical criterion connects it to a judgment.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                The response contains one episode:
                <md>
                  e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
                </md>
                where
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                The word <q>so</q> makes the link explicit.
              </p>
            </li>
            <!-- <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                The episode is a candidate for the working-codebook entry that uses the
                existence of a free variable to judge uniqueness. The match rests on the
                complete criterion-to-judgment relationship, not on the phrase
                <q>free variable</q> alone.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                The criterion is true and the link is valid in the stated context:
                <md>
                  \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                The response answers the uniqueness question. It does not identify
                <m>x_2</m> as the free variable or <m>x_1,x_3</m> as the basic variables.
                Goal coverage is therefore partial.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                This is a clear episode-level case. A reviewer still confirms the working
                codebook match and records partial goal coverage rather than treating
                <m>(1,1)</m> as evidence of a complete answer.
              </p>
            </li> -->
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-incomplete">
      <title>An Incomplete Episode</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              There is a free variable.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>
        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                <c>review</c>. The response is not a complete justification, but its criterion 
                is usable evidence.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                Record one incomplete episode candidate:
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>Not applicable because no criterion-to-judgment move is completed</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>Missing</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                Do not convert the missing judgment into <c>link_stated=absent</c>; the link-status 
                field describes a link between an identifiable criterion and judgment.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Completeness</title>
              <p><c>complete = 0</c> (incomplete).</p>
            </li>
            <!-- <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                The fragment suggests the free-variable family, but it does not support a
                final control-structure code because the student has not drawn a judgment.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                Leave both correctness digits blank. The criterion is true, but incomplete
                episodes do not receive the episode correctness pair.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                The response indicates that some variable is free, but it does not identify
                <m>x_2</m>, identify the basic variables, or address uniqueness. It touches
                one goal without completing it.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Preserve the fragment. Do not add <q>therefore the solution is not
                unique</q> or any other conclusion for the student.
              </p>
            </li> -->
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-chained">
      <title>A Chain of Two Episodes</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              There are fewer pivots than variables, so one variable is
              free. Therefore, the solution is not unique.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>
        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                <c>include</c>. The response contains two connected criterion-to-judgment moves.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                Record the two-episode chain:
                <md>E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2\rangle</md>,
                where <m>e_1</m> has components:
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>fewer pivots than variables</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>an unpivoted variable is free</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>one variable is free</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                and <m>e_2</m> has components:
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_2</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>one variable is free</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_2</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>a free variable rules out uniqueness</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_2</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>the solution is not unique</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                The episodes are chained because <m>J_1=C_2</m>. This would get logged on 
                <m>e_2</m>'s row as <c>chained(1)</c>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <!-- <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                The first episode is a candidate for a pivot-count-to-free-variable control.
                The second is a candidate for a free-variable-to-non-uniqueness control.
                They should not be reduced to one match merely because both involve pivots
                and variables.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                Both criteria are true and both links are valid:
                <md>
                  \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1),
                  \qquad
                  \operatorname{code}(e_2)=(1,1).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                The response answers the uniqueness question and states that one variable is
                free. It does not identify <m>x_2</m> or the basic variables. Coverage is
                partial.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Until the nesting-retention policy is fixed, record both visible episodes
                and their relation. Do not collapse the rows during the independent pass.
              </p>
            </li> -->
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <aside xml:id="aside-example-independent-contrast">
      <title>Contrast: independent episodes</title>

      <p>
        The constructed response
      </p>
      <program>
        A free variable exists, so the solution is not unique. The parametric
        solution also contains a parameter, so the solution is not unique.
      </program>
      <p>
        contains two separate justifications for the same judgment. Neither episode's
        judgment becomes the criterion of the other, so they are independent for
        coding purposes. See <xref ref="subsec-episode-independent" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <example xml:id="example-multiple-controls">
      <title>Several Controls and a Segmentation Choice</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          The source question asks whether the columns of a matrix <m>A</m> span
          <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>, and the matrix has exactly two pivots.
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              2 pivots implies dim(C(A)) = 2, and dim(C(A)) is less than
              dim(R^3), so the columns of A cannot span R^3 by the
              Goldilocks Theorem.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>
        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                <c>include</c>. The response contains a connected mathematical argument and a
                direct answer to the span question.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                One defensible segmentation is
                <md>
                  E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,e_3\rangle,
                </md>
                with
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                <md>
                  \begin{aligned}
                    e_1
                    &amp;=(\text{two pivots},
                        \text{pivot count determines column-space dimension},
                        \dim C(A)=2),\\
                    e_2
                    &amp;=(\dim C(A)=2&lt;3,
                        \text{a two-dimensional subspace cannot equal }\mathbb{R}^3,
                        C(A)\ne\mathbb{R}^3),\\
                    e_3
                    &amp;=(C(A)\ne\mathbb{R}^3,
                        \text{the columns span exactly }C(A),
                        \text{the columns do not span }\mathbb{R}^3).
                  \end{aligned}
                </md>
                A coder could also combine <m>e_2</m> and <m>e_3</m> into one larger
                episode. The difference is a boundary decision, not disagreement about the
                overall argument.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                Plausible matches involve pivot count, dimension or column space, and the
                course-specific theorem named by the student. The theorem label should
                receive a separate code only if the current working codebook defines its
                role and distinguishes it from the dimension argument.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                Given the stated two-pivot context, the pivot and dimension steps are valid.
                Under the three-episode segmentation, each episode receives <m>(1,1)</m>.
                A reviewer should not use the theorem's name alone as proof that the link is
                valid; the actual criterion-to-judgment relation must be checked.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                Complete for the stated span question: the response concludes that the
                columns do not span <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> and provides supporting reasoning.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Record whether the disagreement concerns boundaries, nesting, or code
                assignment. Do not treat a segmentation difference as a screening or
                mathematical-correctness disagreement.
              </p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-oov">
      <title>Relevant but Out-of-Vocabulary Reasoning</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              It depends what is in the augmented matrix.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>

        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                Preserve for review. The response is mathematically relevant and makes a
                conditional claim about what information controls the answer.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                A possible complete reading is
                <md>
                  e_1=(\text{the augmented entries may vary},
                      \text{those entries can change the relevant classification},
                      \text{the answer depends on the augmented matrix}).
                </md>
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                The final judgment is expressed through <q>depends</q>, but the precise
                classification being judged is absent from the preserved context. An
                alternative coding is therefore an ambiguous or incomplete episode. Record
                the primary reading, the alternative, and low confidence.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                Suppose the controlled vocabulary includes pivots, free variables,
                consistency, and solution counts but lacks a proposition for dependence on
                the augmented column. Record the raw phrase as OOV. Do not force the response
                into a pivot or consistency code merely because those ideas are common in
                nearby questions.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                Do not assign the two-digit code from this excerpt alone. The exact question
                and mathematical state are required to determine whether the criterion and
                link are valid.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                Unclear because the requested goal is not preserved with the response.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Decide whether the case is a new expression of an existing idea, a proposed
                vocabulary term, a proposed control, an incomplete episode, or reasoning
                outside the current scope. Until that review occurs, retain the OOV evidence
                and route the case for terminology review or escalation.
              </p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-unresolved">
      <title>A Genuinely Unresolved No-Match Case</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              It is the same because, as we discussed in class, only four
              pivot points would be available.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>
        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                Preserve for review. The response names a mathematical feature and attempts
                to use it as justification, but the referent of <q>the same</q> is missing.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                A tentative representation is
                <md>
                  e_1=(\text{only four pivot positions are available},
                      \text{that limitation leaves the relevant property unchanged},
                      \text{the result is the same}).
                </md>
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                The criterion is identifiable, but both the link and judgment are too vague
                for a defensible final extraction without the question. Record the competing
                interpretation rather than pretending the pronoun has a fixed mathematical
                referent.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                <q>Pivot</q> may already be represented in the controlled vocabulary, so this
                is not automatically OOV. It is a no-match case when no current codebook
                entry adequately represents the unclear pivot-to-<q>same</q> relationship.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                Do not score. The original question is needed to identify the judgment and
                evaluate whether the cited pivot limitation supports it.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                Unresolved because the response's target and the original question goals are
                unavailable in the excerpt.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Preserve the case as unresolved unless the original prompt can be restored.
                Likely disagreement labels include <c>LINK</c>, <c>JUDGMENT</c>,
                <c>MATCH</c>, and possibly <c>POLICY</c>. Missing context is not a license to
                choose the most plausible meaning.
              </p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-invalid-link">
      <title>A True Criterion with an Invalid Link</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              There is a free variable, so the system is inconsistent.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>

        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                <c>include</c>. Codability does not depend on mathematical correctness; the
                response contains a criterion, explicit link, and judgment.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                Record one complete episode:
                <md>
                  e_1=(\text{a free variable exists},
                      \text{a free variable makes the system inconsistent},
                      \text{the system is inconsistent}).
                </md>
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                The response invokes free-variable and consistency reasoning. Preserve the
                student's actual relationship. Do not replace it with the correct
                free-variable-to-non-uniqueness link. The current protocol determines whether
                this receives a control-family code with an incorrect link or a no-match
                designation against the valid question-specific pattern.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                The criterion is true, but the link is invalid because the problem states
                that the system is consistent:
                <md>
                  \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,0).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                The response does not answer the uniqueness question and does not identify
                the variables. Its judgment also contradicts the supplied problem state.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Preserve the distinction between a true observed feature and an invalid use
                of that feature. A single label such as <q>incorrect</q> would erase the main
                analytic result.
              </p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>

    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-partial-coverage">
      <title>Local Reasoning with Partial Goal Coverage</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              We only have two pivot points, so one variable will be free.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>

        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                <c>include</c>. The response contains a criterion, explicit link, and judgment.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                Record
                <md>
                  e_1=(\text{two pivots among three variables},
                      \text{fewer pivots than variables leaves an unpivoted variable},
                      \text{one variable is free}).
                </md>
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                The episode is a candidate for the working-codebook entry that compares
                pivot count with variable count to identify a free variable.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                The criterion is true and the link is valid:
                <md>
                  \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                Partial. The response correctly states that one variable is free, but it
                does not identify <m>x_2</m>, identify <m>x_1,x_3</m> as basic, or state
                whether the solution is unique.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Do not lower the correctness pair because the answer is incomplete. Record
                a sound local episode and partial coverage as separate results.
              </p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-bare-answer">
      <title>A Correct Answer without Codable Justification</title>

      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <stack>
            <p>Response:</p>
            <pre>
              No, the solution is not unique.
            </pre>
          </stack>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <title>Potential Coding</title>

        <p>
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title>Screening</title>
              <p>
                Under a screen requiring identifiable justification, the response is not
                included as a complete episode. Preserve the response and route it according
                to the current bare-answer policy rather than deleting it.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Extraction</title>
              <p>
                Record an incomplete row if the schema requires one:
                <dl width="narrow">
                  <li><title><m>C_1</m>:</title><p>Missing.</p></li>
                  <li><title><m>I_1</m>:</title><p>Not recoverable because no criterion is stated.</p></li>
                  <li><title><m>J_1</m>:</title><p>The solution is not unique.</p></li>
                </dl>
              </p>
              <tabular valign="top">
                <col width="5%" />
                <col width="95%" />
                <row>
                  <cell><p><m>C_1</m>:</p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable exists.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>I_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>A free variable in this consistent system rules out exactly one solution.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell><p><term><m>J_1</m>:</term></p></cell>
                  <cell><p>The solution is not unique.</p></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
              <p>
                Set <c>complete=0</c>. The final answer is not evidence that the student used
                the most obvious available criterion.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Matching</title>
              <p>
                Do not assign a free-variable, pivot-count, or other control code based only
                on the judgment <q>not unique.</q>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Correctness</title>
              <p>
                The final answer is mathematically correct, but no episode-level correctness
                pair is assigned because the response contains no complete episode.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Goal coverage</title>
              <p>
                The uniqueness goal is answered. The response does not identify the basic or
                free variables, so overall coverage is partial.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title>Human decision</title>
              <p>
                Apply the screening and bare-answer rules consistently. Do not infer a hidden
                justification from the fact that the answer is correct.
              </p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-examples-summary">
    <title>Summary of the Examples</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-examples-summary">
      <title>The principal analytic issue in each example.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="10%"/>
        <col width="32%"/>
        <col width="58%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Example</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Main issue</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Central lesson</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Complete episode</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A locally sound episode can still leave question goals uncovered.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>2</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Incomplete fragment</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve a stated criterion without supplying the student's missing judgment.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>3</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Chained episodes</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record each visible step and its relation before applying a retention policy.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>4</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Several controls</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Segmentation and code assignment are distinct from mathematical correctness.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>5</p></cell>
          <cell><p>OOV and ambiguity</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve unfamiliar reasoning and avoid scoring without the question context.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>6</p></cell>
          <cell><p>No match and missing context</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Known vocabulary does not guarantee an adequate codebook relationship.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>7</p></cell>
          <cell><p>True criterion, invalid link</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Criterion truth and inferential validity must be evaluated separately.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>8</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Partial goal coverage</p></cell>
          <cell><p>A <m>(1,1)</m> episode does not certify a complete response.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>9</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Correct bare answer</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Final-answer correctness does not provide evidence for a control structure.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION X: PREVIOUS EXAMPLES                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-exercise-episode-warmup">
    <title>Episode Identification Practice (Original ToDo List)</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The goal is to practice the one skill everything else builds on: read
        a response, break it into <term>episodes</term>: criterion (<m>C</m>),
        inferential link (<m>I</m>), judgment (<m>J</m>), defined in the
        <xref ref="sec-coders-handbook" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-setup">
      <title>The Setup</title>

      <p>
        All five responses answer the same question (actual system omitted):
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <q>Can this system have exactly one solution? Why or why not?</q>
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        For <em>this</em> question, the correct analysis is:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The system is <term>consistent</term> and has <term>more variables
          than equations</term>, so it has a <term>free variable</term>, and
          therefore it has <term>infinitely many solutions</term>. So it
          <em>cannot</em> have exactly one solution.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Treat that as the true state of the mathematics when you judge the
        two correctness digits using
        <xref ref="subsec-handbook-two-digit" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-responses">
      <title>The five responses (made up for this exercise)</title>

      <p>
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>STU-A</title>
            <p><q>
              There is a free variable, so it cannot have exactly one solution.
            </q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-B</title>
            <p><q>
              There are more variables than equations, so there will be a 
              free variable, so it can't have exactly one solution.
            </q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-C</title>
            <p><q>
              There are more variables than equations, so there are infinitely
              many solutions.
            </q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-D</title>
            <p><q>There is a free variable in this system.</q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-E</title>
            <p><q>No.</q></p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-produce">
      <title>What to produce</title>

      <p>
        Use 
        <url href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Ok7EQg3d1Io3B7j5C7ecJ6BbsMmzCdtb/">
          <c>episode-coding-sheet.csv</c>
        </url>
        from the shared folder. <term>One row per episode</term>, so a response with two 
        episodes gets two rows, and a response with none still gets one row recording that.
        Fill these columns; <em>leave <c>code</c>, <c>alt_codes</c>,
        <c>goal_coverage</c>, <c>codebook_version</c>, and
        <c>protocol_version</c> blank</em> (you have neither the codebook nor the
        coding protocol yet — that's deliberate):
      </p>

      <p>
        Fill out all columns except <c>code</c>, <c>alt_codes</c>, <c>goal_coverage</c>,
        <c>codebook_version</c>, and <c>protocol_version</c>. For how to score the 
        <c>confidence</c> column, see
        <xref ref="subsec-handbook-confidence" text="title"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <c>link_recorded</c> is the one that takes practice: write the smallest
        inference that gets you from the criterion to the judgment, in your own
        words. If you genuinely cannot reconstruct one, write <c>[none stated]</c>
        — that does <em>not</em> make the episode incomplete.
      </p>

      <p>
        For clarity, here is a worked row for <term>STU-A</term>:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-warmup-example">
        <title>Worked coding row for STU-A.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="24%"/>
          <col width="76%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Column</cell>
            <cell>Value for STU-A</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>response_id</c></cell>
            <cell>STU-A</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>episode_n</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>criterion_verbatim</c></cell>
            <cell>there is a free variable</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>link_recorded</c></cell>
            <cell>a free variable leaves the solution unconstrained, so it cannot be unique</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>judgment_verbatim</c></cell>
            <cell>it cannot have exactly one solution</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>link_stated</c></cell>
            <cell><c>explicit</c></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>complete</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>c1</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>c2</c></cell>
            <cell>1</cell></row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>relation</c></cell>
            <cell>(blank)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><c>confidence</c></cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><c>notes</c></cell>
            <cell>
              free var is true of this system; free var <m>\Rightarrow</m> not
              exactly one solution is a valid step regardless of consistency
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-watch">
      <title>Things to watch for</title>

      <p>
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>STU-B</title>
            <p>
              More than one episode. Where does one end and the next begin,
              and how do you record that the second one's criterion is the
              first one's judgment?
              (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-nesting" text="title"/>.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-C</title>
            <p>
              The tricky one. Its conclusion is the <em>right answer for this
              system</em>. Ask yourself whether the criterion <em>by 
              itself</em> forces that conclusion, or whether a system with
              <q>more variables than equations</q> could have landed somewhere
              else. Let that decide <m>c^2</m>. Confidence 2 and a note is a
              perfectly good answer here; it's exactly the kind of case we'll
              talk through together.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-D</title>
            <p>
              States something but stops. Is it a complete episode?
              (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-two-digit" text="title"/>, rule 2.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>STU-E</title>
            <p>One row. What can and can't you fill in?</p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ================================================================= -->
    <!-- ANSWER KEY. See the note at the top of this file about hiding this -->
    <!-- subsection from the student-facing build.                          -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-warmup-solution">
      <title>Coding solution</title>

      <p>
        Try the exercise before reading this. One correct coding of each response
        follows. Reasonable coders may differ on <term>STU-C</term> in particular;
        that disagreement is the point, and it belongs on the adjudication queue
        rather than being settled by fiat.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-warmup-solution-summary">
        <title>Answer key at a glance.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="16%"/>
          <col width="60%"/>
          <col width="24%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Response</cell>
            <cell>Episodes and scores</cell>
            <cell>Confidence</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>STU-A</cell>
            <cell>one episode: <m>(1,1)</m></cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>STU-B</cell>
            <cell>two episodes, chained: <m>(1,1)</m>, <m>(1,1)</m></cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>STU-C</cell>
            <cell>one episode: <m>(1,0)</m></cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>STU-D</cell>
            <cell>one row, incomplete (criterion only)</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>STU-E</cell>
            <cell>one row, incomplete (bare answer)</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>STU-A — one episode, <m>(1,1)</m></title>
          <p>
            <m>C</m> = <q>there is a free variable</q>, <m>J</m> = <q>it cannot have
            exactly one solution</q>, link explicit. <m>c^1 = 1</m>: the system does
            have a free variable. <m>c^2 = 1</m>: a free (non-pivot) variable means
            the system is either inconsistent or has infinitely many solutions (in
            neither case exactly one) so the step holds whether or not consistency
            was stated.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>STU-B — two chained episodes, both <m>(1,1)</m></title>
          <p>
            Episode 1: <m>C</m> = <q>there are more variables than equations</q>,
            <m>J</m> = <q>there will be a free variable</q>; <m>c^1 = 1</m>;
            <m>c^2 = 1</m>, because more columns than rows forces a non-pivot column
            regardless of consistency. Episode 2 (<c>relation = chained(1)</c>):
            <m>C</m> = <q>there will be a free variable</q>, <m>J</m> = <q>it can't
            have exactly one solution</q>, scored exactly as STU-A. Record both rows
            and do not collapse them. The nesting policy <m>\pi</m> is still open
            (<xref ref="subsec-handbook-nesting" text="title"/>).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>STU-C — one episode, <m>(1,0)</m> (the teaching case)</title>
          <p>
            <m>C</m> = <q>there are more variables than equations</q>, <m>J</m> =
            <q>there are infinitely many solutions</q>. <m>c^1 = 1</m> (true of this
            system). <m>c^2 = 0</m>: <q>more variables than equations</q> does not
            entail <q>infinitely many solutions</q> (the system could be
            inconsistent, so the criterion does not force that conclusion). The
            conclusion is correct for this particular system, but <m>c^2</m> grades
            the inference, not the answer (resist the halo effect
            of a right answer). This is the response most likely to divide coders on
            how strict to be about the missing consistency premise, so confidence 2
            with a note is the right way to record it, and it goes to adjudication.
            Contrast STU-A: the same family of criterion, but STU-A's weaker
            conclusion (<q>not exactly one</q>) is robust to consistency while
            STU-C's stronger conclusion (<q>infinitely many</q>) is not.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>STU-D — one row, incomplete</title>
          <p>
            <m>C</m> = <q>there is a free variable</q>; no verdict is drawn, so
            <c>complete = 0</c> and <c>c1</c>/<c>c2</c> are blank. A criterion
            with no conclusion is not an episode; do not supply the missing
            judgment. Leave <c>link_stated</c> and <c>link_recorded</c> blank —
            with no judgment there is no step for a link to license, and
            <c>absent</c> means <em>the warrant is unrecoverable</em>, which is a
            different situation (<m>\pi</m> <m>\S</m>4).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>STU-E — one row, incomplete</title>
          <p>
            A bare <q>No</q>: <c>judgment_verbatim</c> = <q>No</q>, no criterion,
            <c>complete = 0</c>, <c>c1</c>/<c>c2</c> blank, and
            <c>link_stated</c>/<c>link_recorded</c> blank for the same reason as
            STU-D. It answers the question but offers no justification structure
            to code.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </subsection>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 6: EXAMPLES                                          -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <!-- <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-examples">
    <title>Worked Examples</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section applies the framework to several short responses. The
        examples are ordered from relatively clear cases to cases requiring more
        human interpretation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each example considers some or all of the following questions:
        <ol>
          <li>Should the response continue past screening?</li>
          <li>How many episodes appear in the response?</li>
          <li>
            What are the criterion, inferential link, and judgment in each
            episode?
          </li>
          <li>How are multiple episodes related?</li>
          <li>Which control-structure codes might apply?</li>
          <li>
            Is the criterion true, and is the inferential link mathematically
            valid?
          </li>
          <li>Does the response address the complete goal of the question?</li>
          <li>What human decision, if any, remains unresolved?</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Some response texts below come from existing project documents or
        published studies. Others are constructed examples designed to isolate a
        particular coding issue. Constructed examples are identified explicitly.
        Student identifiers are omitted because they are not needed for the
        analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        The analyses are intended to demonstrate how the framework is used. A
        final research code should always be based on the current question,
        codebook, controlled vocabulary, nesting policy, and human-review
        protocol.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-context">
      <title>A Common Question Context</title>

      <p>
        Several examples use a question that presents an augmented matrix in
        reduced row echelon form, states that the corresponding system is
        consistent, and asks the student to:
        <ul>
          <li>determine whether the solution is unique; and</li>
          <li>identify the basic and free variables.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose the matrix has three variable columns and two pivot columns. The
        agreed mathematical reference is:
        <ul>
          <li>two variables are basic;</li>
          <li>one variable is free;</li>
          <li>the solution is not unique; and</li>
          <li>
            because the system is known to be consistent, it has infinitely many
            solutions.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        This context is important. The same statement may support different
        conclusions in a different problem.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-complete">
      <title>Example 1: One Complete Episode</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There is a free variable, so the solution is not unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        This is the kind of clean, single-episode justification that passes through 
        all stages of the pipeline with an <c>auto_accept</c> label. Here is the 
        complete process:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>
            <p>
              The response contains a mathematical criterion and connects it to a
              judgment. It should therefore be included for episode analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>
            <p>
              The response contains one complete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>
                  <p>A free variable exists.</p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>
                  <p>
                    A system with a free variable cannot have exactly one solution.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>
                  <p>The solution is not unique.</p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The episode is a candidate for a control structure based on the
              existence of a free variable. In the stated question context:
              <ul>
                <li>the criterion is true; and</li>
                <li>the inferential link is valid.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The local episode would receive
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              This is a relatively clear case. The main human task is to confirm that
              the codebook entry matches the complete criterion-to-judgment
              relationship rather than matching only the phrase
              <q>free variable.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-incomplete">
      <title>Example 2: An Incomplete Episode</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        We cannot determine the value of the free variable.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response is mathematically relevant and provides evidence about
              how the student interprets a free variable. It is not blank,
              unrelated, or merely a bare answer. It should therefore be preserved
              for episode analysis and human review.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response suggests an incomplete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    A free variable is present.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing or insufficiently expressed.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The phrase <q>we cannot determine the value</q> describes something
              the student associates with a free variable, but it does not clearly
              state a judgment about uniqueness, the number of solutions, or the
              complete solution set.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The response may be related to a control structure involving free
              variables. However, the missing inferential link and judgment prevent
              the episode from being matched confidently to a complete
              criterion-to-judgment code.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The episode should not receive a two-part correctness code because
              the inferential link and judgment cannot be identified.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              A reviewer may expect the student to continue with
              <q>therefore, the solution is not unique</q> or
              <q>therefore, there are infinitely many solutions.</q> The response
              does not state either conclusion. The reviewer must preserve the
              episode as incomplete rather than selecting a conclusion for the
              student.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-chain">
      <title>Example 3: A Chain of Two Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Consider the following constructed response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There are fewer pivots than variables, so one variable is free.
        Therefore, the solution is not unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains mathematical criteria, inferential
              connections, and judgments. It should therefore be included for
              episode analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response contains two complete episodes:
              <md>
                E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2\rangle,
              </md>
              where
              <md>
                \begin{aligned}
                  e_1
                  &amp;=
                  (\text{fewer pivots than variables},
                  \text{an unpivoted variable is free},
                  \text{a free variable exists}),\\
                  e_2
                  &amp;=
                  (\text{a free variable exists},
                  \text{a free variable rules out exactly one solution},
                  \text{the solution is not unique}).
                \end{aligned}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              The episodes are chained because the judgment of <m>e_1</m> becomes
              the criterion of <m>e_2</m>:
              <md>
                J_1=C_2.
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The first episode is a candidate for a control structure based on
              comparing the number of pivots with the number of variables. The
              second is a candidate for a control structure based on the existence
              of a free variable.
            </p>

            <p>
              The response should not be assigned only one code merely because both
              episodes involve pivots and variables. The two criterion-to-judgment
              relationships are distinct.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              In the stated question context, both criteria are true and both
              inferential links are valid. Thus,
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1)
                \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                \operatorname{code}(e_2)=(1,1).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must apply the project's nesting policy consistently.
              The two mathematical steps are visible, but the policy determines
              whether both episodes receive separate codes or whether one is
              treated as part of a larger episode.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-multiple-controls">
      <title>Example 4: Several Control Structures in One Response</title>

      <p>
        In a published exam analysis, students were asked whether the columns of a
        matrix span <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        2 pivots implies dim(C(A)) = 2, and dim(C(A)) is less than
        dim(R^3), so the columns of A cannot span R^3 by the
        Goldilocks Theorem.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains several connected mathematical claims and a
              final conclusion. It should therefore be included for episode
              analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              One possible segmentation contains three chained episodes:
              <md>
                E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,e_3\rangle,
              </md>
              where
              <md>
                \begin{aligned}
                  e_1
                  &amp;=
                  (\text{the matrix has two pivots},
                  \text{the number of pivots determines the column-space dimension},
                  \dim(C(A))=2),\\
                  e_2
                  &amp;=
                  (\dim(C(A))=2&lt;3,
                  \text{a two-dimensional subspace cannot equal }\mathbb{R}^3,
                  C(A)\ne\mathbb{R}^3),\\
                  e_3
                  &amp;=
                  (C(A)\ne\mathbb{R}^3,
                  \text{the columns span exactly the column space},
                  \text{the columns do not span }\mathbb{R}^3).
                \end{aligned}
              </md>
            </p>

            <p>
              Another defensible segmentation could combine the final two steps
              into one larger episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The response contains evidence for control structures involving:
              <ul>
                <li>pivots;</li>
                <li>dimension or column space; and</li>
                <li>the Goldilocks Theorem.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              These matches arise from the roles the ideas play in the argument,
              not merely from the presence of the words <q>pivots</q>,
              <q>dimension</q>, and <q>theorem.</q>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              Assuming the matrix has exactly two pivots, the criteria and
              inferential links in the proposed chain are mathematically valid.
              Each complete episode would therefore receive
              <m>(1,1)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              Reviewers may agree on the mathematical argument but disagree about:
              <ul>
                <li>the number of episodes;</li>
                <li>whether some episodes are nested;</li>
                <li>which controls should receive separate codes; or</li>
                <li>
                  whether a theorem-based code includes the intermediate
                  dimension-based reasoning.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              These are segmentation and coding-policy decisions rather than
              disagreements about whether the response contains mathematical
              justification.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-oov">
      <title>Example 5: Relevant but Out-of-Vocabulary Reasoning</title>

      <p>
        For a question involving an augmented matrix, consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        It depends what is in the augmented matrix.
      </program>

      <p>
        This is one of the responses traced through the whole pipeline where its 
        out-of-vocabulary phrasing produces a <c>terminology_flag</c> disposition.
      </p>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response is not blank or unrelated. It expresses a conditional
              claim about the information needed to answer the question. A
              conservative screening decision should preserve it for human review.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              One possible episode is:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The contents of the augmented matrix may vary.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The requested classification depends on information in the
                    augmented column.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The answer cannot be determined from the stated information.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The judgment is not stated directly. A reviewer may instead decide
              that the response contains an incomplete or ambiguous episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              Suppose the controlled vocabulary includes pivots, free variables,
              consistency, and numbers of solutions, but does not include a term
              for reasoning that the answer depends on the augmented column. The
              response should then receive an OOV flag.
            </p>

            <p>
              It should not be forced into a pivot or consistency code merely
              because those ideas may be relevant to the question.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              A correctness code should be assigned only if the reviewer determines
              that the episode is complete and the exact question provides enough
              information to evaluate the criterion and link.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must decide whether the response represents:
              <ul>
                <li>unfamiliar wording for an existing control;</li>
                <li>a new candidate vocabulary term;</li>
                <li>a new control structure;</li>
                <li>an incomplete episode; or</li>
                <li>reasoning outside the current analytic scope.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-no-match">
      <title>Example 6: Extractable Reasoning with No Codebook Match</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        It is the same because, as we discussed in class, only four
        pivot points would be available.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains a mathematical feature and an attempted
              justification. It should therefore be included for analysis, although
              the referent of <q>the same</q> may require the original question to
              interpret.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              One possible episode is:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Only four pivot positions are available.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The limitation on pivot positions leaves the relevant property
                    unchanged.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The relevant mathematical result is the same.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              Both the meaning of <q>the same</q> and the precise inferential link
              remain unclear without the original question.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The response may use vocabulary already represented in the project,
              such as pivots, while still failing to match an existing codebook
              rule. This would be a <term>no-match</term> case rather than
              necessarily an OOV case.
            </p>

            <p>
              In an OOV case, the mathematical idea itself is missing from the
              controlled vocabulary. In a no-match case, the vocabulary may
              represent the idea, but the codebook contains no adequate
              criterion-to-judgment classification.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The episode should not receive a correctness code until the original
              question clarifies the judgment and the mathematical relationship
              being asserted.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer should determine whether the difficulty results from:
              <ul>
                <li>missing question context;</li>
                <li>an incomplete inferential link;</li>
                <li>an inadequate codebook;</li>
                <li>an incorrect extraction; or</li>
                <li>a genuinely new control structure.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-invalid-link">
      <title>Example 7: A True Criterion with an Invalid Link</title>

      <p>
        Consider the following constructed response:
      </p>

      <program>
        There is a free variable, so the system is inconsistent.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains a criterion, an inferential link, and a
              judgment. It should therefore be included for episode analysis even
              though the conclusion is mathematically incorrect.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response contains one complete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    A free variable exists.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    A system with a free variable is inconsistent.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The system is inconsistent.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The episode is a candidate for a control structure involving free
              variables and consistency. The match should preserve the student's
              actual criterion-to-judgment relationship rather than replacing it
              with the correct relationship between free variables and
              non-uniqueness.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              In the stated question context:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  the criterion is true because a free variable exists; but
                </li>
                <li>
                  the inferential link is invalid because a consistent system may
                  have a free variable and infinitely many solutions.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              Therefore,
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,0).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer should preserve the distinction between identifying a
              true mathematical feature and using that feature incorrectly.
              Labeling the entire response only as <q>incorrect</q> would hide this
              distinction.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-goal-coverage">
      <title>Example 8: Sound Local Reasoning but Incomplete Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        We only have two pivot points, so one variable will be free.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response contains a criterion, an inferential link, and a
              judgment. It should therefore be included for episode analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response contains one complete episode:
              <md>
                e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
              </md>
              where
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    There are two pivots among three variables.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Fewer pivots than variables leaves at least one variable
                    unconstrained.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    One variable is free.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The episode is a candidate for a control structure based on comparing
              the number of pivots with the number of variables.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              In the given matrix, the criterion is true and the inferential link is
              valid. Therefore,
              <md>
                \operatorname{code}(e_1)=(1,1).
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Goal coverage</title>

            <p>
              The response does not complete all parts of the prompt:
              <ul>
                <li>it does not state whether the solution is unique;</li>
                <li>it does not identify which variable is free;</li>
                <li>it does not identify the basic variables; and</li>
                <li>
                  it does not explicitly state the number of solutions.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
              The response therefore contains sound local justification but only
              partial goal coverage.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must not lower the episode's correctness code merely
              because the response is incomplete. Episode correctness and goal
              coverage are separate annotations.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-example-bare-answer">
      <title>Example 9: A Correct Answer without Codable Justification</title>

      <p>
        Consider the response:
      </p>

      <program>
        No, the solution is not unique.
      </program>

      <p>
        We process the response as follows:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Screening</title>

            <p>
              The response states a judgment but does not provide a mathematical
              criterion or an inferential link. Under a screening policy requiring
              identifiable justification, it should be excluded from episode
              analysis or routed according to the project's bare-answer policy.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Extraction</title>

            <p>
              The response provides:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title><m>C_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>I_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    Missing.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <title><m>J_1</m>:</title>

                  <p>
                    The solution is not unique.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              Because the criterion and inferential link are absent, the response
              does not contain a complete codable episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Matching</title>

            <p>
              The phrase <q>not unique</q> may resemble the judgment used in a
              free-variable control structure, but there is no evidence that the
              student actually used a free variable, a pivot count, or any other
              particular criterion.
            </p>

            <p>
              The response should therefore not be assigned a control-structure
              code based only on the final answer.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Correctness</title>

            <p>
              The final answer is mathematically correct in the stated question
              context. However, no episode-level correctness code can be assigned
              because the response does not provide a complete episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Human decision</title>

            <p>
              The reviewer must apply the screening policy consistently and avoid
              inferring that the student used the most obvious correct
              justification. Final-answer correctness and codable justification are
              separate properties.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-examples-summary">
      <title>Summary of the Examples</title>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-examples-summary">
        <title>The principal analytic issue illustrated by each example.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="12%"/>
          <col width="34%"/>
          <col width="54%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Example</cell>
            <cell>Main issue</cell>
            <cell>Central lesson</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>Complete single episode</cell>
            <cell>
              A criterion, link, and judgment can appear clearly in one sentence.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>Incomplete episode</cell>
            <cell>
              Do not supply a missing link or judgment for the student.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>Chained and nested reasoning</cell>
            <cell>
              Episode existence and episode-coding policy are separate
              questions.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>Several control structures</cell>
            <cell>
              Multiple mathematical ideas require analysis of their roles, not
              keyword counting.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>Out-of-vocabulary reasoning</cell>
            <cell>
              Relevant unfamiliar reasoning should be preserved rather than
              forced into an existing term.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>No codebook match</cell>
            <cell>
              Extractable reasoning may still fall outside current codebook
              rules.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>7</cell>
            <cell>True criterion, invalid link</cell>
            <cell>
              Criterion correctness and inferential validity must be evaluated
              separately.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>Partial goal coverage</cell>
            <cell>
              Sound local reasoning does not guarantee a complete answer.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>9</cell>
            <cell>Correct but non-codable answer</cell>
            <cell>
              Final-answer correctness does not provide evidence for a control
              structure.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section> -->

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 7: PRACTICE EXERCISES                                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <!-- <section xml:id="sec-student-guide-practice">
    <title>Practice Exercises</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The exercises in this section prepare student researchers to analyze
        actual project data. They begin with small recognition tasks and build
        toward complete response coding and calibration.
      </p>

      <p>
        The exercises use synthetic responses written to resemble the kinds of
        short answers found in the project data. They are not responses from the
        research corpus.
      </p>

      <p>
        These exercises restate, in a self-contained form, the rules the
        project's separate <term>Coder's Handbook</term> lays out as the working
        manual. When you begin real coding, that handbook and its coding-sheet
        template are the authority for the exact columns you fill and the values 
        they take.
      </p>

      <p>
        The exercises have four purposes:
        <ol>
          <li>
            practice applying the terminology introduced in this guide;
          </li>
          <li>
            identify parts of the coding protocol that remain unclear;
          </li>
          <li>
            help student researchers become consistent with one another; and
          </li>
          <li>
            produce a record of disagreements that can improve the codebook and
            training materials.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Uncertainty should be recorded rather than hidden. In research coding, an
        accurately described ambiguity is more useful than an unsupported
        confident answer.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-procedure">
      <title>How to Complete the Exercises</title>

      <p>
        For the introductory exercises, students may discuss their reasoning
        while working. For the calibration exercises near the end of the section,
        each student must work independently before comparing results.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unless an exercise gives different directions, use the following
        procedure:
        <ol>
          <li>Read the complete question.</li>
          <li>Read the student response exactly as written.</li>
          <li>
            Record what is explicit, what is strongly supported, and what is
            missing or ambiguous.
          </li>
          <li>
            Apply the current coding rules without silently repairing the
            response.
          </li>
          <li>Give a short rationale tied to visible evidence.</li>
          <li>
            Mark any decision for which the current guide or codebook does not
            provide enough direction.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not consult another student's coding during an independent exercise.
        Independent first-pass coding is necessary if the group later wants to
        measure agreement or identify genuine differences in interpretation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-context">
      <title>Common Mathematical Context</title>

      <p>
        Most of the exercises in this section use the following linear algebra
        question.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-student-guide-practice-question">
        <title>Practice Question A</title>

        <p>
          The reduced row echelon form of the augmented matrix of a system in the
          variables <m>x_1,x_2,x_3</m> is
          <md>
            \left[
            \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2  &amp; 3\\
              0 &amp; 1 &amp; -1 &amp; 4\\
              0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0  &amp; 0
            \end{array}
            \right].
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          Is the system consistent? Does it have a unique solution? Identify the
          basic and free variables. Explain your reasoning.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        The agreed mathematical reference for this question is:
        <ul>
          <li>the system is consistent;</li>
          <li><m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are basic variables;</li>
          <li><m>x_3</m> is a free variable; and</li>
          <li>the system has infinitely many solutions and is not unique.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The reference is supplied because these exercises concern coding, not
        testing the researcher's linear algebra knowledge.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-identify">
      <title>Part I: Identify the Parts of an Episode</title>

      <p>
        For each response below:
        <ol>
          <li>identify the criterion <m>C</m>;</li>
          <li>identify the inferential link <m>I</m>;</li>
          <li>identify the judgment <m>J</m>; and</li>
          <li>
            classify each component as explicit, strongly supported, or missing.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A1.</term>
            <q>
              There is no contradictory row, so the system is consistent.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            One possible analysis is:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <term>Criterion:</term> there is no contradictory row;
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Inferential link:</term> an augmented matrix with no
                contradictory row represents a consistent system; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Judgment:</term> the system is consistent.
              </li>
            </ul>
            All three components are explicit or strongly supported.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A2.</term>
            <q>
              Since the third column does not contain a pivot, <m>x_3</m> is
              free.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            One possible analysis is:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <term>Criterion:</term> column three contains no pivot;
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Inferential link:</term> a variable corresponding to a
                non-pivot column is free; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Judgment:</term> <m>x_3</m> is free.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A3.</term>
            <q>
              There is a free variable, so the solution is not unique.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            One possible analysis is:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <term>Criterion:</term> a free variable exists;
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Inferential link:</term> a system with a free variable
                cannot have exactly one solution; and
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Judgment:</term> the solution is not unique.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-4">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A4.</term>
            <q>
              There are two pivots.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            The response states a mathematical feature, so it provides a possible
            criterion. It does not state what judgment follows from that
            criterion. The inferential link and judgment are therefore missing.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-identify-5">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response A5.</term>
            <q>
              <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are basic variables.
            </q>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <answer>
          <p>
            The response states a judgment, but it does not explain the criterion
            or inferential link used to reach it. A coder should not automatically
            add <q>because columns one and two contain pivots</q>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-operation-control">
      <title>Part II: Distinguish Operations from Controls</title>

      <p>
        For each statement, classify it as:
        <ul>
          <li>an operation;</li>
          <li>a control;</li>
          <li>both an operation and a control;</li>
          <li>insufficient to determine; or</li>
          <li>neither.</li>
        </ul>
        Explain the classification in one or two sentences.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-operation-control-1">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li><q>I row reduced the matrix.</q></li>
            <li>
              <q>
                The last row is all zeroes, so it does not create a
                contradiction.
              </q>
            </li>
            <li>
              <q>
                Columns one and two contain pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and
                <m>x_2</m> are basic.
              </q>
            </li>
            <li><q>I got the matrix above.</q></li>
            <li>
              <q>
                There is a free variable, so there are infinitely many
                solutions.
              </q>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-operation-control-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why the statement
            <q>I found two pivots</q>
            may require additional context before it can be classified.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-screen">
      <title>Part III: Screen Responses</title>

      <p>
        For each response:
        <ol>
          <li>
            propose <c>INCLUDE</c>, <c>EXCLUDE</c>, or human review according to
            the current project policy;
          </li>
          <li>give a one-sentence rationale; and</li>
          <li>
            state whether your decision depends on a policy that the group still
            needs to settle.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-screening-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S1.</term>
                <q>No.</q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S2.</term>
                <q>
                  There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S3.</term>
                <md>
                  x_1=3-2x_3,\qquad x_2=4+x_3.
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S4.</term>
                <q>
                  The matrix has two pivots and three variables. Therefore,
                  <m>x_3</m> is free and the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S5.</term>
                <q>
                  I do not understand what the question is asking.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S6.</term>
                <q>
                  It depends.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S7.</term>
                <q>
                  The answer is not unique because the third variable can take
                  any value.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response S8.</term>
                <em>No response was submitted.</em>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-screening-policy">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare Responses S3 and S7. Both contain mathematical information,
            but only one explicitly connects that information to a conclusion.
            Explain how the current screening policy should distinguish them.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-segment">
      <title>Part IV: Segment Responses into Episodes</title>

      <p>
        For each response:
        <ol>
          <li>determine how many episodes are present;</li>
          <li>write each episode as <m>(C,I,J)</m>;</li>
          <li>
            classify the relationship among the episodes as chained,
            independent, nested, or unresolved; and
          </li>
          <li>
            identify any decision that depends on the project's nesting policy.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E1.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              There are only two pivots for three variables, so one variable is
              free. Since a free variable can take different values, the solution
              is not unique.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E2.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              There is no contradictory row, so the system is consistent. Also,
              there is a free variable, so it has infinitely many solutions.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E3.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              Columns one and two have pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are
              basic. Column three does not have a pivot, so <m>x_3</m> is free.
            </p>
          </blockquote>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-segment-4">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Response E4.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              The system is consistent and <m>x_3</m> is free, which means the
              solution set is a line and therefore there are infinitely many
              solutions.
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            Give at least two plausible segmentations. State which one you prefer
            and what additional coding rule would make the choice more
            consistent.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-codebook">
      <title>Part V: Match Episodes to a Practice Codebook</title>

      <p>
        The following codes are simplified practice codes. They are not the
        official project code IDs and should not be used when coding research
        data.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-practice-codebook">
        <title>Miniature codebook for Practice Question A.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="15%"/>
          <col width="23%"/>
          <col width="36%"/>
          <col width="26%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Code</cell>
            <cell>Name</cell>
            <cell>Include when</cell>
            <cell>Do not include when</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC1</c></cell>
            <cell>Contradictory-row consistency</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses the presence or absence of a contradictory row to
              classify the system as consistent or inconsistent.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The response merely performs row reduction without interpreting a
              row.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC2</c></cell>
            <cell>Pivot-variable classification</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses pivot or non-pivot columns to classify variables
              as basic or free.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Pivots are mentioned but no variable classification is made.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC3</c></cell>
            <cell>Free-variable uniqueness</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses the existence or absence of free variables to
              decide whether the solution is unique.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              A free variable is identified but no conclusion about uniqueness
              or number of solutions is drawn.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>PC4</c></cell>
            <cell>Pivot-count comparison</cell>
            <cell>
              The response compares the number of pivots with the number of
              variables to infer that a free variable exists or that every
              variable is basic.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              The response only states the number of pivots without making the
              comparison or drawing the relevant judgment.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><c>PC5</c></cell>
            <cell>Geometric solution-set interpretation</cell>
            <cell>
              The response uses the dimension or geometric shape of the solution
              set to justify uniqueness or multiplicity.
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Geometric language is used descriptively but does not support a
              judgment.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        For each response below:
        <ol>
          <li>extract the episode or episodes;</li>
          <li>record every plausible practice code;</li>
          <li>identify the textual evidence supporting each match;</li>
          <li>identify any plausible near match that should be rejected; and</li>
          <li>give a short matching rationale.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-practice-match-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M1.</term>
                <q>
                  Column three has no pivot, so <m>x_3</m> is free.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M2.</term>
                <q>
                  There are two pivots but three variables, so one variable must
                  be free.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M3.</term>
                <q>
                  One variable is free, so the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M4.</term>
                <q>
                  There is no row that says zero equals a nonzero number, so the
                  system is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M5.</term>
                <q>
                  The solutions form a line because there is one parameter.
                  Therefore, there are infinitely many solutions.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response M6.</term>
                <q>
                  I found two pivots.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-practice-keyword-warning">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why the word <q>pivot</q> is not sufficient by itself to
            assign either <c>PC2</c> or <c>PC4</c>. Use Responses M1, M2, and M6
            in your explanation.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-correctness">
      <title>Part VI: Evaluate Criterion and Link Correctness</title>

      <p>
        For each complete episode:
        <ol>
          <li>
            determine whether the criterion is true in Practice Question A;
          </li>
          <li>
            determine whether the inferential link validly supports the judgment;
          </li>
          <li>
            assign the pair <m>(c^1,c^2)</m>; and
          </li>
          <li>
            explain the two digits separately.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Use:
        <ul>
          <li><m>c^1=1</m> when the criterion is true;</li>
          <li><m>c^1=0</m> when the criterion is false;</li>
          <li><m>c^2=1</m> when the inferential link is valid; and</li>
          <li><m>c^2=0</m> when the inferential link is invalid.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-correctness-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C1.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_3</m> is free, so the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C2.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_3</m> is free, so the solution is unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C3.</term>
                <q>
                  There are three pivots, so every variable is basic and the
                  solution is unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C4.</term>
                <q>
                  The matrix contains a contradictory row, so the system is
                  inconsistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C5.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_1</m> is free, so the system is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response C6.</term>
                <q>
                  There are two pivots.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-correctness-incomplete">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why Response C6 should not receive a two-digit correctness
            code under the current Episode Model.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-correctness-final-answer">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Identify one response in the preceding list that reaches an incorrect
            judgment using a true criterion, and one that uses a valid general
            inferential link with a false criterion. Explain why these are
            different errors.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-goal">
      <title>Part VII: Evaluate Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        Practice Question A asks for four pieces of information:
        <ol>
          <li>whether the system is consistent;</li>
          <li>whether the solution is unique;</li>
          <li>which variables are basic; and</li>
          <li>which variables are free.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        For each response below:
        <ol>
          <li>identify every episode;</li>
          <li>evaluate the local justification;</li>
          <li>list which parts of the prompt are addressed; and</li>
          <li>
            classify goal coverage as complete, partial, or absent.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-goal-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G1.</term>
                <q>
                  There is no contradictory row, so the system is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G2.</term>
                <q>
                  Columns one and two contain pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and
                  <m>x_2</m> are basic. Column three does not contain a pivot, so
                  <m>x_3</m> is free. Since a free variable exists, the solution
                  is not unique. The system is consistent because there is no
                  contradictory row.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G3.</term>
                <q>
                  <m>x_3</m> is free, so the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G4.</term>
                <q>
                  The system is consistent and has infinitely many solutions.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response G5.</term>
                <q>
                  There are two pivots, so <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are free.
                  Therefore, the solution is not unique.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-goal-quality">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare Responses G3 and G5. One contains a sound local episode but
            incomplete goal coverage. The other reaches a correct conclusion
            through an incorrect variable classification. Explain why the two
            responses must not receive the same analytic description.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-oov">
      <title>Part VIII: Identify OOV and No-Match Cases</title>

      <p>
        Use the miniature practice codebook from Part V. For each response:
        <ol>
          <li>extract any identifiable episode;</li>
          <li>determine whether an existing practice code fits;</li>
          <li>
            distinguish unfamiliar wording from a genuinely new reasoning type;
          </li>
          <li>
            classify the case as an existing match, OOV wording, no match,
            incomplete, or unresolved; and
          </li>
          <li>recommend what a reviewer should do next.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-oov-set">
        <statement>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O1.</term>
                <q>
                  The third variable has room to move, so there cannot be only one
                  answer.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O2.</term>
                <q>
                  The solution set has one degree of freedom, so it is a line
                  rather than a point.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O3.</term>
                <q>
                  The bottom row does not break the system.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O4.</term>
                <q>
                  The third column is dependent on the first two, so the system
                  has infinitely many solutions.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Response O5.</term>
                <q>
                  The matrix is wider than it is tall, so this particular system
                  is consistent.
                </q>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-oov-proposal">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Select one response that appears to require a vocabulary or codebook
            revision. Draft:
            <ol>
              <li>a proposed canonical term or code name;</li>
              <li>a one-sentence definition;</li>
              <li>an inclusion rule;</li>
              <li>an exclusion rule; and</li>
              <li>a reason the proposal should or should not be adopted.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-review">
      <title>Part IX: Review a Proposed Pipeline Analysis</title>

      <p>
        In these exercises, the pipeline has supplied a proposed analysis.
        Review the proposal rather than beginning from scratch.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-review-packet-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Original response.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              There are two pivots for three variables, so <m>x_3</m> is free
              and the solution is not unique.
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            <term>Pipeline proposal.</term>
            <ul>
              <li><term>Screening:</term> <c>INCLUDE</c></li>
              <li>
                <term>Episode 1:</term>
                two pivots
                <m>\longrightarrow</m>
                <m>x_3</m> is free
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Episode 2:</term>
                <m>x_3</m> is free
                <m>\longrightarrow</m>
                solution is not unique
              </li>
              <li>
                <term>Candidate codes:</term>
                <c>PC2</c>, <c>PC3</c>, <c>PC4</c>
              </li>
              <li><term>Disposition:</term> standard review</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Review every proposed component. Confirm, revise, or reject each
            decision and provide a rationale.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-review-packet-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <term>Original response.</term>
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            <p>
              The third variable can change, so there are multiple answers.
            </p>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            <term>Pipeline proposal.</term>
            <ul>
              <li><term>Screening:</term> <c>EXCLUDE</c></li>
              <li><term>Rationale:</term> no mathematical keyword found</li>
              <li><term>Episodes:</term> none</li>
              <li><term>Disposition:</term> escalate</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Review the proposal. Explain why keyword absence is or is not an
            adequate screening rationale.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-review-anchoring">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Recode the two original responses without looking at the proposed
            pipeline outputs. Compare the unaided result with the review you made
            after seeing the proposals. Did the proposal influence any decision?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-calibration">
      <title>Part X: Independent Calibration</title>

      <p>
        Calibration is the process of checking whether coders apply the analytic
        rules consistently. Calibration is not achieved merely by discussing
        examples together. Coders must first analyze the same responses
        independently.
      </p>

      <p>
        The research lead will provide a calibration set containing a mixture of:
        <ul>
          <li>clear single-episode responses;</li>
          <li>blank and bare-answer responses;</li>
          <li>incomplete episodes;</li>
          <li>multi-episode responses;</li>
          <li>nested or chained reasoning;</li>
          <li>multiple-match cases;</li>
          <li>OOV and no-match cases;</li>
          <li>
            responses with a true criterion but invalid inferential link; and
          </li>
          <li>
            responses with sound local reasoning but incomplete goal coverage.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-first-pass">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Independently code every response in the supplied calibration set.
            For each response, record:
            <ol>
              <li>screening decision and rationale;</li>
              <li>number of episodes;</li>
              <li><m>C</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>J</m> for each episode;</li>
              <li>relationship among episodes;</li>
              <li>candidate control-structure codes;</li>
              <li>criterion and link correctness, when applicable;</li>
              <li>goal coverage;</li>
              <li>OOV, no-match, or ambiguity flags;</li>
              <li>confidence or uncertainty notes; and</li>
              <li>recommended disposition.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-compare">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare the independent results. For every disagreement, identify the
            earliest point in the workflow at which the codings diverged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Assign one or more of the following disagreement types:
            <ul>
              <li><c>SCREEN</c>: screening decision;</li>
              <li><c>BOUNDARY</c>: number or boundary of episodes;</li>
              <li><c>CRITERION</c>: interpretation of <m>C</m>;</li>
              <li><c>LINK</c>: interpretation of <m>I</m>;</li>
              <li><c>JUDGMENT</c>: interpretation of <m>J</m>;</li>
              <li><c>RELATION</c>: chained, independent, or nested;</li>
              <li><c>MATCH</c>: candidate code assignment;</li>
              <li><c>CORRECTNESS</c>: criterion or link correctness;</li>
              <li><c>COVERAGE</c>: completeness relative to the prompt;</li>
              <li><c>VOCABULARY</c>: OOV or canonical-term decision;</li>
              <li><c>POLICY</c>: different coding conventions applied; or</li>
              <li><c>OTHER</c>: disagreement not captured above.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-adjudicate">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Adjudicate each disagreement by:
            <ol>
              <li>returning to the original question and response;</li>
              <li>stating the evidence for each interpretation;</li>
              <li>consulting the current guide and codebook;</li>
              <li>reaching agreement when the evidence supports a decision;</li>
              <li>
                preserving the case as unresolved when the evidence is
                insufficient; and
              </li>
              <li>
                recording whether the disagreement requires a revision to the
                coding materials.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-calibration-revise">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Select one recurring disagreement. Draft a revision to the relevant
            definition, policy, example, or exclusion rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test the proposed revision on:
            <ul>
              <li>the response that produced the disagreement;</li>
              <li>at least two similar responses; and</li>
              <li>at least one near-miss response that should not receive the same
              treatment.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Record whether the revision:
            <ul>
              <li>resolves the original disagreement;</li>
              <li>creates a new ambiguity;</li>
              <li>changes any previously coded response; and</li>
              <li>should be adopted, revised further, or rejected.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-record">
      <title>Calibration Record</title>

      <p>
        The calibration process should produce a durable record rather than only
        a verbal discussion. Use a table with at least the following fields.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-student-guide-calibration-record">
        <title>Recommended fields for documenting a calibration disagreement.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="24%"/>
          <col width="76%"/>

          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Field</cell>
            <cell>What to record</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Response identifier</cell>
            <cell>
              The stable practice or research identifier, not the student's name.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Decision level</cell>
            <cell>
              Screening, segmentation, component interpretation, matching,
              correctness, goal coverage, or disposition.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Coder A decision</cell>
            <cell>The first coder's independent result and rationale.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Coder B decision</cell>
            <cell>The second coder's independent result and rationale.</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Evidence</cell>
            <cell>
              The exact wording, notation, or feature supporting each
              interpretation.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Disagreement type</cell>
            <cell>
              The category from the disagreement taxonomy.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Adjudicated decision</cell>
            <cell>
              The agreed result or a statement that the case remains unresolved.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Reason for decision</cell>
            <cell>
              A concise explanation tied to the question, response, and current
              coding rule.
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Follow-up action</cell>
            <cell>
              No change, add an example, clarify a definition, revise a policy,
              propose a vocabulary term, or revisit previous responses.
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-final">
      <title>Final Onboarding Task</title>

      <p>
        After completing the guided exercises, each student researcher will
        independently analyze a small onboarding set selected by the research
        lead. This set should not contain responses reserved for the formal
        validation study.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-student-guide-final-onboarding">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each response in the onboarding set, prepare a complete analysis
            packet containing:
            <ol>
              <li>the original question and response identifier;</li>
              <li>screening decision and rationale;</li>
              <li>episode sequence <m>E(x)</m>;</li>
              <li>
                criterion, inferential link, and judgment for each episode;
              </li>
              <li>evidence supporting each component;</li>
              <li>episode relationships and nesting-policy decisions;</li>
              <li>candidate codebook matches and rationales;</li>
              <li>correctness annotations for complete episodes;</li>
              <li>goal-coverage assessment;</li>
              <li>uncertainty, OOV, or no-match flags;</li>
              <li>recommended disposition; and</li>
              <li>a short reviewer note explaining the most difficult decision.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        The research lead will review the packets and identify:
        <ul>
          <li>decisions the student can already make independently;</li>
          <li>decisions requiring additional practice;</li>
          <li>coding rules that remain unclear; and</li>
          <li>
            response types that should be discussed before the student begins
            coding project data.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-student-guide-practice-readiness">
      <title>Readiness Checklist</title>

      <p>
        Before beginning independent work with project data, a student researcher
        should be able to:
        <ul>
          <li>
            distinguish a response-level episode from a claim about a complete
            conception;
          </li>
          <li>
            screen a response without confusing codability with correctness;
          </li>
          <li>identify explicit, supported, and missing episode components;</li>
          <li>distinguish operations from enacted controls;</li>
          <li>segment common chained and independent responses;</li>
          <li>apply the current nesting policy consistently;</li>
          <li>match episodes using definitions rather than isolated keywords;</li>
          <li>
            evaluate criterion correctness separately from inferential-link
            correctness;
          </li>
          <li>evaluate goal coverage separately from local justification;</li>
          <li>recognize OOV, no-match, and unresolved cases;</li>
          <li>write concise evidence-based rationales;</li>
          <li>record disagreement without treating it as failure;</li>
          <li>participate in adjudication using the original evidence; and</li>
          <li>
            recommend a clarification when a coding rule does not adequately
            resolve a case.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section> -->

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      These examples show why the project records several decisions rather than one
      overall label. A response may be codable but incorrect, locally sound but
      incomplete, mathematically relevant but outside the vocabulary, or impossible to
      interpret without its original question. The coder's responsibility is to preserve
      those distinctions, apply the governing versions consistently, and leave a case
      unresolved when the evidence does not support a unique decision.
    </p>
  </conclusion>

</chapter>